{"verdict":"approved","issues":[{"severity":"minor","file":"src/hooks/useContent.ts","line":7,"description":"The plan marked useContent.ts as NO EDIT REQUIRED, prescribing that the existing useContext(ContentstackContext) would automatically pick up reactive updates once an updater was added to IContentstackContext. The implementer instead created a separate LivePreviewContext, which necessitated this 7-line modification to merge the two contexts. If ContentstackContext.Provider is indeed in _app.tsx as the plan states, the plan's approach (add updatePage callback to IContentstackContext, manage page state in _app.tsx, pass setter into provider value) would have been more concise and avoided touching this file. However, if the provider is not in _app.tsx (the diff context lines show no ContentstackContext.Provider visible), the separate-context approach is justified.","suggestedFix":"If ContentstackContext.Provider is in _app.tsx: revert useContent.ts, add updatePage?: (page: IContentstackPage[]) => void to IContentstackContext, manage page state via useState in _app.tsx, pass setter into the existing ContentstackContext.Provider value, and call it from onEntryChange. If the provider is elsewhere: the current approach is acceptable."},{"severity":"minor","file":"src/context/contentstackContext.tsx","line":24","description":"previewPage is typed as [IContentstackPage] (a tuple type with exactly one element) but the existing page field in IContentstackContext is IContentstackPage[] (an array type). While setPreviewPage([result]) satisfies both types at runtime, the tuple type is semantically incorrect for a field that represents page data. It should match the existing page field's array type for consistency.","suggestedFix":"Change ILivePreviewContext.previewPage type from [IContentstackPage] to IContentstackPage[] to match the existing IContentstackContext.page type."},{"severity":"minor","file":"src/services/contentstack.ts","line":53","description":"The `as contentstack.Config[\"live_preview\"]` cast is used to bypass the SDK's LivePreview interface which requires `host: string`. While the explanatory comment is appreciated, the cast silences the type checker for the entire object — any future field type errors would also be suppressed. A narrower approach would be safer.","suggestedFix":"Construct the object in two steps: first build the base with enable and management_token, then conditionally assign host: `const livePreview: contentstack.Config['live_preview'] = { enable: true, management_token: CONTENTSTACK_PREVIEW_TOKEN } as contentstack.Config['live_preview']; if (CONTENTSTACK_PREVIEW_HOST) { livePreview.host = CONTENTSTACK_PREVIEW_HOST; }` — or use a Partial type and spread, keeping the cast scope as narrow as possible."},{"severity":"minor","file":"src/pages/_app.tsx","line":33","description":"The StackSdkInitParam conditional type extraction is clever but hard to read. It infers the stackSdk parameter type from ContentstackLivePreview.init's parameter shape, then uses it for the `as unknown as StackSdkInitParam` bridge. This complexity is necessary because the live-preview-utils SDK's stackSdk type is structurally incompatible with the contentstack SDK's Stack type, but a simpler documented assertion would be more maintainable.","suggestedFix":"Replace the StackSdkInitParam type with a direct assertion and comment: `stackSdk: Stack as unknown as Parameters<typeof ContentstackLivePreview.init>[0]['stackSdk'],` or simply `// SDK type mismatch: live-preview-utils expects IStackSdk, contentstack exports Stack — same instance at runtime` with `stackSdk: Stack as unknown as NonNullable<Parameters<typeof ContentstackLivePreview.init>[0]>['stackSdk']`."}],"summary":"The implementation addresses the prescribed root cause for Story AMSD-2041 (Live Preview of Content in CMS). The live_preview configuration block is correctly added to the contentstack Stack options (src/services/contentstack.ts), using management_token (which is the correct key per the contentstack SDK's LivePreview interface — the plan's reference to preview_token was incorrect). ContentstackLivePreview.init and onEntryChange are wired up in _app.tsx with proper useEffect scoping (client-side only) and a .catch() handler for Promise rejection. The onEntryChange callback correctly reuses getSingleEntry for client-side re-fetching of draft entries, and the data-cslp DOM attribute pattern is the standard Contentstack Live Preview mechanism for identifying edited entries.\n\nThe main deviation from the plan is the creation of a separate LivePreviewContext instead of adding an updater callback to IContentstackContext. This necessitated modifying useContent.ts (marked NO EDIT REQUIRED in the plan) to merge the two contexts. If ContentstackContext.Provider is in _app.tsx as the plan states, the plan's approach would have been more concise. However, the diff context lines show no ContentstackContext.Provider visible in _app.tsx, suggesting it may be elsewhere — in which case the separate-context approach is justified.\n\nNo secrets are pasted into the code (CONTENTSTACK_PREVIEW_TOKEN and CONTENTSTACK_PREVIEW_HOST are process.env references). No hardcoded model names, endpoint URLs, or file paths. No path traversal or injection concerns. Error handling is present. The CONTENTSTACK_PREVIEW_HOST addition (not in the plan) is a reasonable configurability improvement. All acceptance criteria appear addressable by this implementation: draft entries are fetched and pushed into reactive state, non-preview behavior is preserved (previewPage is undefined when no signal is active), and protected/gated pages are handled implicitly (no data-cslp elements means the callback returns early)."}
